ervices, new exposure, package damage, or abandoned temporary files. Reboot-related PID and systemd-private-directory changes are expected lifecycle effects.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9e9134328c5f4096","cmd-e2b2f099a96045bc","cmd-367eccb7d06c4ac8","cmd-3dc6263894984886","cmd-846c4ad4bb3b4566","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Direct before-and-after behavior proves usb-storage is blocked immediately and after a genuine reboot, while the operating-system release and kernel remained unchanged. The restart protocol is coherent: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and bounded loss of connectivity, changed boot identity, and recovered running state with a read-write root filesystem, retained configuration, expected sockets, and healthy package state.","limitations":["No application-specific workload or cluster service was identified in the single-node contract, so restart recovery evidence is limited to system health, root filesystem availability, retained configuration, module behavior, listening services, and package health."]}
